1
00:00:00,390 --> 00:00:05,550
Japan began with gods, monsters,
and four brothers who set out to rule it.

2
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:08,740
Only one of them survived.

3
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:16,140
That was Prince Iwarebiko, who became
Japan's first emperor, if he ever existed.

4
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:21,440
First, an arrow struck the eldest brother.

5
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:26,340
The arrow wouldn't come out,
and he died far from home.

6
00:00:28,060 --> 00:00:30,040
A storm took the other two.

7
00:00:31,740 --> 00:00:36,860
In 2019,
Iwarebiko's heir received a sacred sword.

8
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:40,680
Legend says it came from a serpent's tail.

9
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:43,400
So how did it reach the emperors?

10
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:49,980
It all starts when the young earth
still drifted like a jellyfish.

11
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:58,900
Two gods, Izanagi and Izanami, came down
the bridge of heaven to give it shape.

12
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:03,004
They dipped a jewelled spear into the sea

13
00:01:03,024 --> 00:01:05,940
and stirred
until the salt water began to thicken.

14
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:11,150
When they lifted it,
salt dripped from the tip,

15
00:01:11,170 --> 00:01:14,040
and from that drop grew the first island.

16
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:18,100
The god and the goddess settled there,

17
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:21,440
and eight great islands were born to them:
Japan.

18
00:01:26,060 --> 00:01:30,370
Her last child was the god of fire,
who burned her so badly

19
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:33,520
that she died
and went down to the land of the dead.

20
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:40,400
Izanagi followed her into the darkness,
but when he saw what she had become,

21
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:43,560
he fled
and sealed the entrance with a boulder.

22
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:50,190
Then he washed in a river:
from his left eye came Amaterasu,

23
00:01:50,210 --> 00:01:53,390
the sun goddess
and ancestor of the four brothers,

24
00:01:53,410 --> 00:01:58,560
from his right the moon god,
and from his nose Susanoo, god of storms.

25
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:05,020
Susanoo raged across heaven,
wrecked his sister's rice fields,

26
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:09,020
and finally smashed the roof of the hall
where she was weaving.

27
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:15,480
Offended,
Amaterasu shut herself inside a rock cave,

28
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:18,360
and darkness fell over heaven and earth.

29
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:26,520
The gods set roosters before
the cave to crow as if at dawn,

30
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:29,020
and hung a mirror and jewels on a tree.

31
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:33,061
Then a goddess danced on an upturned tub

32
00:02:33,081 --> 00:02:36,560
so wildly
that all the gods burst out laughing.

33
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:43,480
Curious, Amaterasu peeked out at her own
radiance in the mirror, and that mirror

34
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:48,780
became the first of the three sacred
treasures of Japan's emperors.

35
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:57,480
Susanoo was banished to earth,
to the land of Izumo,

36
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:00,460
where chopsticks came
floating down the river.

37
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:05,080
Upstream, an old couple was weeping:
an eight-headed serpent

38
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:10,240
had eaten seven of their daughters
and was coming for the last.

39
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:15,790
Susanoo turned the girl into a comb,
tucked it into his hair,

40
00:03:15,810 --> 00:03:18,640
and set out eight vats of strong sake.

41
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:22,580
The serpent had eight heads
and eight tails,

42
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:26,020
and it stretched across eight valleys
and eight hills.

43
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:34,510
When the drunken heads fell asleep,

44
00:03:34,530 --> 00:03:38,180
Susanoo cut the serpent apart
and found a sword in its tail;

45
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:43,120
tradition says it still lies hidden
in a shrine, and no one alive has seen it.

46
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:52,399
Amaterasu gave the mirror, the jewels
and the sword to her grandson Ninigi,

47
00:03:52,419 --> 00:03:57,820
but the man who would build
a country with them had not yet been born.

48
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:03,909
Ninigi came down from heaven
through eight layers of clouds

49
00:04:03,929 --> 00:04:08,420
to the top of Mount Takachiho,
on the island of Kyushu.

50
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:17,390
Ninigi's great-grandson, Prince Iwarebiko,

51
00:04:17,410 --> 00:04:20,680
grew up on Kyushu,
far from the other islands.

52
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:25,620
When he was forty-five,
he heard that in the east lay

53
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:29,040
a beautiful land surrounded
by blue mountains.

54
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:33,520
It seemed the best place
to rule the whole country from,

55
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:36,680
so with his brothers
and his army he set out east.

56
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:41,250
They sailed along the Inland Sea,

57
00:04:41,270 --> 00:04:45,160
and the chronicle says they stopped
for whole years along the way.

58
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:54,590
At last they reached Naniwa Bay,
where Osaka stands today,

59
00:04:54,610 --> 00:04:57,760
and marched across
the mountains toward Yamato.

60
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:05,300
Their way was blocked by a local chief,
Nagasunehiko,

61
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:08,560
'the Prince with Long Shins',
and his army.

62
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:15,450
Remember that arrow?

63
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:17,500
It struck the eldest brother, Itsuse,

64
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:22,700
and suddenly Iwarebiko
understood why they were losing.

65
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:29,730
As children of the sun goddess,
they had been fighting facing the sun,

66
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:34,040
so they decided to circle the mountains
and attack with the sun behind them.

67
00:05:37,060 --> 00:05:41,740
Itsuse never recovered, and he died
on the boat as they sailed south.

68
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:49,260
Then a storm sank two boats,
and the sea took two more brothers.

69
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:56,440
Iwarebiko had lost all three brothers,
and the hardest part was still ahead.

70
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:08,790
In the Kumano forests,
a huge bear appeared and vanished,

71
00:06:08,810 --> 00:06:12,740
and the whole army collapsed
as if under a spell.

72
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:19,390
Then a local man brought
Iwarebiko another sword,

73
00:06:19,410 --> 00:06:22,840
one the thunder god had dropped
through the roof of his storehouse.

74
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:29,310
When Iwarebiko took the sword,
the evil powers of the forest fell,

75
00:06:29,330 --> 00:06:32,140
and his warriors woke one after another.

76
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:39,260
Heaven also sent a great crow, Yatagarasu,

77
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:43,940
which guided the army through wild
mountains all the way to Yamato.

78
00:06:47,460 --> 00:06:49,560
Later it was painted with three legs,

79
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:53,660
and Japan's national football team
still wears it on its shirts.

80
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,990
In Yamato every hill had its own chief,

81
00:07:01,010 --> 00:07:04,760
and none of them meant to give
his land to a newcomer from Kyushu.

82
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:08,880
One of them built his guest
a house with a hidden trap,

83
00:07:08,900 --> 00:07:13,240
but his own brother gave him away,
and the chief died in his own trap.

84
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:21,484
Iwarebiko fought Nagasunehiko once more,
but the battle dragged on,

85
00:07:21,504 --> 00:07:24,940
the sky grew dark, and hail began to fall.

86
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:34,320
Then a golden bird landed on the tip of
Iwarebiko's bow, shining like lightning.

87
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:40,648
In its memory,
for half a century Japan honoured

88
00:07:40,668 --> 00:07:43,720
its soldiers with the Order
of the Golden Kite.

89
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:47,760
Nagasunehiko still refused to give in,

90
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:52,600
because he served Nigihayahi,
another descendant of the gods.

91
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:58,370
But Nigihayahi decided that the newcomer
had the better claim to rule,

92
00:07:58,390 --> 00:08:01,540
killed the stubborn chief,
and went over to his side.

93
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:11,332
At Kashihara, below a green mountain,
Iwarebiko built a palace whose pillars,

94
00:08:11,352 --> 00:08:15,120
the chronicle says,
were driven into the bedrock.

95
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:23,060
On the first day of the new year,
in 660 BC,

96
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:26,980
he took the throne there
as the first emperor of Japan.

97
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:31,920
The name Jimmu, meaning 'divine valour',

98
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:35,820
was given to him by scholars only
fourteen hundred years later.

99
00:08:38,260 --> 00:08:41,990
The chronicle says the emperor died
at a hundred and twenty-seven,

100
00:08:42,010 --> 00:08:44,680
and the three treasures passed to his son.

101
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:55,610
With him, tradition says, began
a line of emperors unbroken to this day.

102
00:08:55,630 --> 00:08:58,200
But did he ever really exist?

103
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:08,070
But this story was written
down only in 712 and 720,

104
00:09:08,090 --> 00:09:12,280
in chronicles the court ordered
to prove its right to rule.

105
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:18,151
The date 660 BC was probably
worked out from a Chinese prophecy

106
00:09:18,171 --> 00:09:22,160
of a great change every twelve
hundred and sixty years.

107
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:29,146
Meanwhile, in 660 BC, the islands
were home to hunters and fishermen,

108
00:09:29,166 --> 00:09:33,580
and rice farming had only
just reached the southwest.

109
00:09:35,940 --> 00:09:37,490
But in 1784, a farmer repairing

110
00:09:37,510 --> 00:09:42,340
a ditch in his field struck
something golden with his hoe.

111
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:49,080
It was a seal the emperor of China sent
to a king of the Wa in the year 57:

112
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:53,280
proof that real kings ruled the islands
long before the chronicles.

113
00:09:57,420 --> 00:10:00,950
Chinese records of that time
describe the land of the Wa

114
00:10:00,970 --> 00:10:03,240
as over a hundred little states.

115
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:09,076
In the third century, Queen Himiko
ruled there; she was said to keep

116
00:10:09,096 --> 00:10:14,180
the chiefs in line with sorcery, and China
sent her a hundred bronze mirrors.

117
00:10:17,140 --> 00:10:21,430
Later, the rulers of Yamato were
buried in huge keyhole-shaped mounds,

118
00:10:21,450 --> 00:10:24,200
and the largest is almost half
a kilometre long.

119
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:29,930
On their slopes stood thousands of clay
figures called haniwa, and inside

120
00:10:29,950 --> 00:10:35,560
lay real rulers of Yamato, though no
one can say for sure who they were.

121
00:10:42,460 --> 00:10:47,010
In the late seventh century,
the court renamed the country Nihon,

122
00:10:47,030 --> 00:10:52,020
'origin of the sun', because from China,
that is where the sun rose.

123
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:58,190
Only in 1873 did Tokyo convert
the chronicle's date to our calendar

124
00:10:58,210 --> 00:11:00,710
and make the eleventh of February
a holiday,

125
00:11:00,730 --> 00:11:05,120
abolished after the war
and restored in 1966.

126
00:11:06,860 --> 00:11:10,670
In 2019, the hundred and twenty-sixth
emperor was enthroned,

127
00:11:10,690 --> 00:11:14,730
and the jewels and a stand-in for the
serpent's sword were brought to him,

128
00:11:14,750 --> 00:11:17,440
wrapped in cloth no one opens.

129
00:11:20,900 --> 00:11:23,270
No one knows whether Jimmu really lived,
but the country

130
00:11:23,290 --> 00:11:29,020
that began with a drop from a spear still
calls itself the origin of the sun.

131
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:36,800
Thank you for watching! If you like my
videos, support me, and I'll do my best.

